        "Pavlov showed that a neutral signal paired repeatedly with food came to trigger salivation in dogs: this is classical conditioning, learning by stimulus substitution. In classrooms the mechanism still works. A bell becomes a signal for movement, a stern tone signals trouble, and a warm greeting signals a safe room. Teachers of {S} use it deliberately when they pair a calm cue, such as a raised hand, with the learned response of silence, until the cue alone produces quiet.",
        "Thorndike studied cats escaping from puzzle boxes and proposed three laws. The law of readiness: learners respond well when prepared for the act. The law of exercise: connections strengthen with practice and weaken with disuse. The law of effect: responses followed by satisfying consequences are stamped in, while annoying outcomes weaken them. Modern teaching keeps the law of effect at its centre, which is why prompt marking, feedback and recognition matter more than mere repetition, a priority {S} repeats in every methods course.",
        "Skinner worked with rats and pigeons pressing levers and defined operant conditioning: behaviour is shaped by what follows it. Consequences that increase behaviour are reinforcers; schedules matter, because a fixed ratio pays after a set number of responses, a variable ratio pays unpredictably and resists extinction, a fixed interval pays after a set time, and a variable interval pays at irregular times. Variable ratio schedules explain why surprise rewards, like unexpected stars, keep pupils working longest, a device used across {S} classrooms.",
        "Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant after a behaviour, such as praise or points. Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant, such as cancelling homework after steady effort, and it too strengthens behaviour. Punishment is different: it adds or removes something specifically to reduce a behaviour. The classic exam trap is calling negative reinforcement punishment; it is not, it is reinforcement. Skilled teachers reinforce desired behaviour far more often than they punish unwanted behaviour, the balance every student of {S} must defend.",
